WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.660
This robot was tasked to clean up this table.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.700
Normally, anyone who watches this series knows
that would be no big deal for any modern learning

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.009
algorithm.

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:15.650
Just grab it, right?

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:21.949
Well, not in this case, because reason number
one: several objects are tightly packed together,

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:25.820
and reason number two, they are too wide to
hold with the fingers.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:31.009
What this means is that the robot needs to
figure out a series additional actions to

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:35.370
push the other pieces around and finally,
grab the correct one.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.370
Look!

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.489
It found out that sometimes, pushing helps
grasping by making space for the fingers to

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:42.949
grab these objects.

00:00:42.949 --> 00:00:48.150
This is a bit like the Roomba vacuum cleaner
robot, but even better, for clutter.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.360
Really cool.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.590
This robot arm works the following way: it
has an RGB-D camera, which endows it with

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.329
the ability to see both color and depth.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:03.609
Now that we have this image, we have not one,
but two neural networks looking at it: one

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:08.950
is used to predict the utility of pushing
at different possible locations, and one for

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:09.950
grasping.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.810
Finally, a decision is made as to which motion
would lead to the biggest improvement in the

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.310
state of the table.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.040
So, what about the training process?

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.730
As you see, the speed of this robot arm is
limited, and we may have to wait for a long

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:29.060
time for it to learn anything useful and not
just flail around destroying other nearby

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.060
objects.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.530
The solution includes my favorite part - training
the robot within a simulated environment,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:40.720
where these commands can be executed within
milliseconds, speeding up the training process

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:41.720
significantly.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:47.690
Our hope is always that the principles learned
within the simulation applies to reality.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:48.850
Checkmark.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.480
The simulation is also very useful to make
comparisons with other state of the art algorithms

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.480
easier.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.340
And, do you know what the bane of many-many
learning algorithms is?

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.560
Generalization.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.590
This means that if the technique was designed
well, it can be trained on matte looking,

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:10.690
wooden blocks, and it will do well when it
encounters new objects that are vastly different

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.730
in shape and appearance.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.769
And as you see on the right, remarkably, this
is exactly the case.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:17.769
Checkmark.

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:22.950
This takes us one step closer to learning
algorithms that can see the world around us,

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.879
interpret it, and make proper decisions to
carry out a plan.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:30.009
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

